{"role":"assistant","content":"Done. The job was never broken as a scheduler — `launchctl print` shows it ran all 6 nights — it failed silently every time. Full write-up is in `ANSWER.md`; summary:\n\n**Two causes, both visible in `Library/Logs/nightly-review.err` (same 2 lines × 6 runs):**\n\n1. **The script aborted before it could report anything.** In zsh, `status` is a read-only special variable (alias for `$?`). The assignment `status=$?` at `bin/nightly-review.zsh:19` fails with `read-only variable: status`, which is fatal in a non-interactive script — the shell exits 1 right there, so the `notify.sh` branch below never ran. I reproduced this byte-for-byte with the original script and a failing `claude` stub: exit 1, notifier never invoked. This alone explains \"not even a failure.\"\n2. **`claude` isn't logged in under launchd.** It prints `Not logged in · Please run /login` — your interactive login doesn't carry over to the launchd-spawned process (keychain/session credentials). So the job would have failed every night regardless.\n\n**What I changed (in the snapshot only):**\n- `bin/nightly-review.zsh:23` — `status` → `rc`, so the failure path actually reaches the notifier.\n- `bin/nightly-review.zsh:11` — sources `~/.config/nightly-review.env` if present, a clean place for a headless token (deliberately not the plist, since `launchctl print` leaks a job's environment).\n- `bin/notify.sh:4` — if the keychain read fails, it now says so explicitly on stderr instead of dying quietly under `set -eu`.\n\n**Verified end-to-end** in a sandboxed `$HOME` with stub `claude`/notifier: failing claude now sends `nightly review FAILED (exit 1)...`; a working claude sends the summary and writes the dated report.\n\n**One thing only you can do:** the fix makes failures *reportable*, but the reviews themselves need a headless credential — run `claude setup-token`, then put `export CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=...` into `~/.config/nightly-review.env` (chmod 600). Until then you'll at least get a failure note every night instead of silence. I couldn't test the real `security`/Telegram/keychain path from the snapshot, so if failure notes arrive but summaries don't, the `tg-bot` keychain item's access settings are the next suspect (also noted in `ANSWER.md`)."}
